Web Application Security Testing
Project Overview

This project demonstrates basic web application security testing using Python and Jupyter Notebook. It focuses on identifying common security weaknesses through controlled local simulations.

Objectives
Understand basic web application security.
Test authentication and password strength.
Demonstrate SQL Injection.
Demonstrate Cross-Site Scripting (XSS).
Perform input validation checks.
Analyze HTTP security headers.
Perform basic session security checks.
Technologies Used
Python 3.11
Jupyter Notebook
SQLite3
Pandas
Matplotlib
Requests
ReportLab
Security Tests Performed
Test	Description
Authentication Testing	Weak password and login validation
SQL Injection	Vulnerable query demonstration and parameterized query remediation
XSS Testing	Unsafe HTML input and output encoding
Input Validation	Invalid and oversized input checks
Security Headers	Analysis of sample HTTP security headers
Session Security	Session ID uniqueness and logout simulation
Vulnerability Findings
ID	Vulnerability	Risk Level
VULN-001	Insecure Password Storage	High
VULN-002	SQL Injection Simulation	High
VULN-003	XSS Simulation	High
VULN-004	Input Validation Observation	Medium
VULN-005	Missing Security Headers Observation	Medium
VULN-006	Session Security Assessment	Informational
Project Files
Web_Application_Security_Testing.ipynb – Main Jupyter Notebook
Web_Security_Vulnerability_Report.xlsx – Excel report
Web_Security_Vulnerability_Report.csv – CSV report
Web_Application_Security_Report.pdf – Final PDF report
How to Run
Install Python 3.11.
Install Jupyter Notebook.
Install the required Python libraries.
Open the .ipynb file.
Execute notebook cells sequentially.
Disclaimer

This project uses controlled local Python simulations for educational purposes. It is not a penetration test of a production web application. Findings represent simulated vulnerabilities or basic observations, not independently verified production security issues.

